// Wavefront — 2D accumulated-cost matrix (DTW / Smith-Waterman shape).
//
// Reads an HxW i32 cost array `in_cost`, computes the accumulated-cost
// matrix `out_cost` where
//
//   out_cost[i][j] = in_cost[i][j]
//                  + min(out_cost[i-1][j],
//                        out_cost[i][j-1],
//                        out_cost[i-1][j-1])
//
// with boundary conditions:
//
//   out_cost[0][0] = in_cost[0][0]
//   out_cost[0][j] = in_cost[0][j] + out_cost[0][j-1]      (j > 0)
//   out_cost[i][0] = in_cost[i][0] + out_cost[i-1][0]      (i > 0)
//
// This is the canonical 2D wavefront: every interior cell depends on
// its NW, N, W neighbours — three earlier diagonals' worth of
// computation. Natural sequential evaluation is row-major (each cell
// reads cells already written); natural parallel evaluation is
// anti-diagonal (all cells with i+j == k are mutually independent,
// diagonals execute in increasing k).
//
// PRD §9 row 10: "Diagonal dependency, ordering, no SIMD." The
// recurrence is scalar-dependent on its neighbours; vectorisation is
// intentionally hard.
//
// Why ONE big kernel and not three accumulator passes
// ----------------------------------------------------
// The natural in-array carried recurrence
//
//   out[i][j] <-- step(out[i-1][j], out[i][j-1], out[i-1][j-1], in[i][j])
//
// is NOT expressible in Nuc v2 — same fundamental limit hit by
// 04-prefix-sum (TASK-0179):
//   1. `out[(i-1) as usize]` underflows at i=0; v2 has no conditionals
//      (PRD §6.2.4) to special-case the boundary.
//   2. Splitting as `out[0][..] <-- init; for i:1..H { out[i][..] <-- step(...) }`
//      is a DoubleAssignment on `out` (single-assignment per data SYMBOL,
//      not per index — PRD §6.2.1).
//   3. The accumulator trick that saved 04-prefix-sum (push the
//      boundary mask into a Rust kernel, keep the algorithm a
//      rectangular reduction) does NOT apply here: prefix-sum is a
//      sum over a triangular subset that re-expresses as a
//      rectangular reduction; the wavefront recurrence is a
//      MIN-OF-THREE, not a sum. There is no rectangular reduction
//      form.
//
// The honest v2 encoding is therefore: ONE kernel call that takes
// `in_cost` and returns the whole `out_cost`. The recurrence lives
// in `kernels::wavefront` (real Rust, real per-cell sequential
// reads). The algorithm declares only the dataflow:
//
//   in_cost  <-- load_input();
//   out_cost <-- wavefront(in_cost);
//   save_output(out_cost);
//
// The cost: there is no algorithm-level decomposition handle the
// schedule sublanguage can use to express a diagonal-parallel
// schedule. The diagonal-parallel schedule is GRAMMAR-DEFERRED
// (filed as a follow-up); v2 only ships the naive schedule.
//
// Numeric choice: i32 with `wrapping_add`. PRD §10.1 — integer
// arithmetic is bit-deterministic across schedules and backends;
// reorderable floating-point min/sum is not. `wrapping_add` makes
// overflow behaviour explicit; the committed fixture (see README)
// keeps the running cost in-range. `min` is bit-deterministic by
// language definition.

const H : usize = 16;       // wavefront height
const W : usize = 16;       // wavefront width

// Whole-program input/output. Single-assignment per PRD §6.2.1.
data in_cost  : i32[H][W];
data out_cost : i32[H][W];

// I/O kernels — effectful, ordering preserved, never duplicated.
// Aggregate types; on the Rust side they use `Vec<i32>` (see
// kernels.rs and the README's "Why Vec<i32>" section, which sidesteps
// TASK-0103).
kernel load_input  : ()                -> i32[H][W]   effectful;
kernel save_output : (i32[H][W])       -> ()          effectful;

// Pure compute kernel. Takes the whole input cost array, returns the
// whole accumulated cost array. The recurrence lives entirely in
// kernel Rust; the algorithm sees only the dataflow edge `in_cost ->
// wavefront -> out_cost`. Aggregate-typed both sides (TASK-0012
// TypeMismatch surface, identical to examples 01..07).
kernel wavefront   : (i32[H][W])       -> i32[H][W]   pure;

// Dataflow: load, compute, save. Three statements; no loops. The
// algorithm/schedule split here reduces to "which worker runs the
// load / compute / save" — no decomposition axis is exposed at the
// algorithm level (would require grammar extensions for the
// in-array carry; see header).
in_cost  <-- load_input();
out_cost <-- wavefront(in_cost);
save_output(out_cost);
